 normie and high-tier normie.[1] The system descends from a rating scale developed on defunct forums.[1]","Wikipedia's article on looksmaxxing describes seven categories in the PSL (Perceived Sexual Levels) scale, which rates facial attractiveness on a 1 to 8 scale and is built around assumptions about how women perceive men, with the lowest category called 'subhuman' or 'sub-3'.[1] Another description of the scene, associated with the streamer Clavicular, uses three tiers: subhuman, normie and Chad.[1] Researchers and critics consider parts of the PSL system pseudoscientific.[1]"],"infobox":[["Type","Community rating vocabulary"],["Scale","PSL, 1 to 8 (facial attractiveness)"],["Origin","PSL forums (PUAHate, Sluthate, Lookism.net)"],["Top / bottom terms","Chad, giga Chad / subhuman, sub-3"]],"sections":[{"h":"Definitions","paras":["Chad is slang from manosphere forums for an attractive, sexually successful man, and appears as a rank in PSL categories including 'Chadlite' and 'giga Chad'.[1][2] Normie describes an average-looking person; the spelled-out forms low-tier normie and high-tier normie subdivide the middle range.[1][2] Subhuman denotes the lowest PSL category.[1] Stacy and Becky are the corresponding terms for attractive and less attractive women.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Origins","paras":["The vocabulary and rating scale were developed on forums named in coverage as PUAHate.com, Sluthate.com and Lookism.net, collectively the PSL forums, which are now defunct.[1] Looksmaxxing itself was spread to wider audiences by TikTok in the early 2020s.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Criticism and expert views","paras":["Writer Becca Rothfeld criticised, in The New Yorker in March 2026, the practice of labelling any person subhuman.[1] The scheme is regarded by researchers as lacking scientific grounding, in part because it rates only the face and is based on assumed female preferences.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,